  "query_preview": "..\nTuesday evening: Spotty light rain will continue over Northeast\nWA and North ID as a mid- level wave tracks southward toward \nNorthern California. While weak radar echoes can be seen over \nmuch of the region, measurable precip is mainly being observed \nover areas of higher terrain with surface observations \nindicating dry conditions elsewhere. So far precip has been \nlight with this wave as expected. Observed precip amounts over \nthe North Cascades and other areas of higher terrain along the \nCanadian border range from a couple hundredths of an inch to \njust over a tenth of an inch as of 12PM PDT. There is one \nexception - an observation site on the north end of the \nSinlahekin fire perimeter - that has received over a quarter of \nan inch of rain with isolated heavier showers right along and \nnorth of the Canadian border.\n\nWednesday through Monday: By Wednesday the mid-level wave forms\na closed low and moves south of the region, settling over\nNorthern California. Meanwhile, higher heights nudge in over \nEastern WA and North ID, prompting a warming and drying trend. \nThe dry weather will be briefly interrupted Thursday evening \ninto Friday as the low wobbles back to the north, then tracks \nnortheastward toward Montana, bringing a 30-70 percent chance \nof showers and a 20 percent chance of thunderstorms over\nSoutheast WA and the Central ID Panhandle. Heading into the\nweekend and early next week, the low exits the area to the east,\nand ridging develops across the region favoring continued warm \nand dry conditions. Afternoon highs Wednesday through Monday \nwill range from the 70s to low 80s, and overnight lows will \nfall into the 40s to low 50s. Overall, weather through early\nnext week will be typical of mid- September with minimal \nimpacts expected. /Fewkes",
